  "api_error_status": null,
  "result": "**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` rejected prices with thousands separators. A price like `\"2,450.50\"` or `\"1,299.00\"` failed the regex, and the code then read `m[1]` on the null match. That produced the `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')` in the log. I checked all 37 checkout 500s in `logs/app.log`. Every one has a comma-grouped price in its payload, and no other error type appears. That is why it looked intermittent: only orders with an item priced 1,000 or more failed.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- `parseAmount` now accepts properly grouped thousands (`1,234,567.89`) as well as plain digits.\n- Any other input now throws the documented `Error('Invalid amount: …')` instead of a `TypeError`.\n\n**Tests (`test/money.test.js`):**\n- Comma-separated amounts parse correctly.\n- Malformed input (`''`, `1,23.00`, `1.234`, etc.) throws the descriptive error.\n- `checkout` works end to end with a comma-formatted price.\n\n`npm test` passes, 7 of 7. I didn't run the new tests against the old code.\n\nI haven't touched `src/server.js`. If you want malformed prices to return a 4xx instead of a 500, the handler would need to catch the `Invalid amount` error. I haven't looked at how it handles errors.",
